  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Fog and low stratus will impact travel conditions through\n  Wednesday morning across the region. \n\n- The remnants of an atmospheric river will move into the region\n  on Thanksgiving causing some precipitation and potential travel\n  impacts at the mountains. \n\nA ridge is building over the region today causing more stable\nconditions to set up through mid-week. However, the mountain top\nwinds under the building ridge will be maintain around 20 to 25\nmph, helping to keep some turbulent mixing going. This will help\nto break down the mid-level stratus clouds over the region and\npush them out of the area. As a result, expect decreasing cloud\ncover the next couple of days. Those winds won't make it down to\nthe valley floors though, and without the cloud cover tonight\nwe'll see more fog development in the valleys. \n\nLate Wednesday the remnants of an atmospheric river reach the\nregion causing increasing clouds, increasing winds, and some\nprecipitation. Confidence in all of this is high, but there's low\nconfidence about what happens to the cooler air at the surface.\nModels typically push the cold pools out too quickly, but even\nwithout considering that, there is high uncertainty in how low\nsnow levels will go. As this will be the remnants of a dying\natmospheric river, it won't have as much wind to provide a strong\npush. The NBM, a blend of models, is highly uncertain on the snow\nlevels around Missoula for example with snow levels from 0ft to\n4,000 ft in elevation by midnight Thursday (Thanksgiving). Taking\ninto account the typical model biases with valley cold pools and\nthe upper end of the uncertainty in the snow levels, we expect\nmainly wet snow in the valleys of western Montana on Thanksgiving.\nConsidering that even mountain locations like Lookout Pass have\nonly a 25 percent chance of 2 or more inches of snow, we expect\nonly minor accumulations and low to moderate impacts to travel in\nthe valleys on Thanksgiving. \n\nFriday and beyond the weather pattern remains active with several\nperiods of snow showers expected. Accumulations will be only a few\ninches a day in the mountains and spotty accumulations of a couple\ninches a day in some valleys. Forecast temperatures indicate that\nmost of the snow in the valleys will melt each day. So overall,\nonly nuisance to minor impacts are expected through the weekend.\nAs always, when traveling at night during periods of recent\nprecipitation, stay alert for patches of ice.",
